# Reproduce: scripts/npm-install-in-docker.sh
# Host: Ubuntu 22.04 x86_64, Docker 29.8.1. Needs network to registry.npmjs.org.
# Repo HEAD at the time of the run: 88903bf7 (the script is uncommitted in this run).
# Result: 12 of 13 checks passed. The 13th fails, and that failure is the point -- see
# "the one red check" below.

== why this ran in a container

`chaos-code` on npm is a meta package: a JS trampoline plus six optional
platform-specific packages that each carry the real binary. All of it had only been
exercised by `scripts/ci/test-publish-npm.sh`, which hands the publish script directories
of fake tarballs and checks what it would upload. Nothing had asked the public registry
for the package and run what came back. A container is the only way to make that claim
mean something, because the install has to happen on a machine with no `~/.npm` cache,
no repo, and no host mirror -- this host's npm is pointed at `registry.npmmirror.com`, so
an install run here would have tested a mirror, not the registry every user hits.

The container's own configured registry is printed as the first line for that reason:
`https://registry.npmjs.org/`.

