  13 check(s) run, at least one failed

== what the green half establishes

`npm install -g chaos-code` from the public registry works on a clean Linux box, and the
thing that runs afterwards is the platform package's binary rather than the JS trampoline:
exactly one platform package landed, it is the one for this machine's `os`/`cpu`, the
payload directory under it is non-empty, and `chaos --version` reports
`chaos 0.2.110 (4055b47)` -- a build string, which the trampoline could not have produced.
`chaos doctor --json` also starts and prints JSON, so this is not only the `--version`
fast path.

Two facts worth recording rather than asserting: npm serves **0.2.110** while the
repository is at **0.4.2**, so the published npm path is roughly two minors behind the
GitHub releases; and the platform package nests under the meta package's own
`node_modules`, which is why an `npm ls`-based check is useless here (`npm ls --json`
lists declared-but-skipped optional dependencies, so it reports all six platforms as
though they were installed -- the installed directory tree is what can answer the
question).

== the one red check, and why it stayed red

The meta package declares all six platform packages as optional dependencies pinned to its
own version. Two of those names are held by npm's own security placeholders:

  chaos-code-linux-x64    latest 0.2.110   (0.2.109, 0.2.110)
  chaos-code-linux-arm64  latest 0.2.110   (0.2.110)
  chaos-code-darwin-x64   latest 0.2.110   (0.2.110)
  chaos-code-darwin-arm64 latest 0.2.110   (0.2.110)
  chaos-code-win32-x64    latest 0.0.1-security   (0.0.1-security only)
  chaos-code-win32-arm64  latest 0.0.1-security   (0.0.1-security only)

So `chaos-code-win32-x64@0.2.110`, the exact string the meta package pins, has never
existed. npm treats an unresolvable *optional* dependency as skippable, which is the
whole problem: asked to resolve for Windows the install reports success --

  npm install -g --prefix /tmp/win32-x64-prefix --os=win32 --cpu=x64 chaos-code
  -> "added 2 packages", exit 0, no platform package on disk (vs. "added 3 packages"
     with one, for the host platform)

-- and the failure only surfaces when the user runs `chaos`:

  chaos-code: no platform binary installed for win32-x64.
    Expected sibling package chaos-code-win32-x64@0.4.2.
    ...
        npm view chaos-code-win32-x64 versions

That output is from the shipped launcher with only node's `process.platform`/`arch`
overridden, since a Windows host is not available here; the resolution logic, the message
and the exit code are the shipped file's. `crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/npm/chaos/scripts/test-postinstall.js`
runs the real `bin/chaos` in a child node and asserts the report names the platform, names
the pinned version, prints the `npm view` command that settles it, exits 1, and is not a
stack trace. Deleting the pinned version from the message turns that test red, so the
assertion is load-bearing.

Before this round the launcher's message read "this usually means npm skipped
optionalDependencies (e.g. --no-optional) or the platform is not supported" -- both wrong
diagnoses for the actual cause here, which is that the version was never published under
that name. That is why the message now names the pinned version and gives the
`npm view` command.

The check was left failing rather than downgraded to a note. It is the same condition
`release.yml` already refuses to publish under

  ::error::npm package name $reserved is reserved by npm security placeholder;
  resolve it before enabling npm publication.

so a permanently-red check here is consistent with a workflow that already blocks on it.
Unblocking needs an external action -- an npm support request to reclaim the two names, or
a decision to rename the Windows packages and migrate the pin -- neither of which can be
done from this machine.

== what this file is NOT evidence about

- That `npm install -g chaos-code` works on Windows or macOS. Only Linux x86_64 was
  installed and executed. The Windows case was resolved *for* win32-x64 with npm's
  `--os`/`--cpu` filters and then run through the launcher under a stubbed platform, which
  shows what the launcher does when its sibling package is absent; it is not a Windows
  install.
- That 0.4.2 can be published to npm. Nothing was published here, and the reserved-name
  problem above would hit the next publish of either Windows package.
- That the npm payload is signed or checksum-verified. This run checked that the install
  produced a working binary. Signature handling on the npm path is a separate claim; the
  signed-GitHub-release path is `docs/verification/release-signature-v0.4.2-2026-10-02.log`.
- Anything about `update` behaviour through npm (`GROK_MANAGED_BY_NPM` is set by the
  launcher, but no update was performed).


